In Python, OOP is highly dominant, but it is heavily blended with Functional Programming. Python treats everything as an object, but its modern ecosystem uses Data-driven OOP rather than traditional, rigid Java-style OOP.
In the specific stack of FastAPI, LangChain, and LangGraph, the architectural paradigm shifts from traditional classes to a hybrid model focused on data schemas, immutability, and state configuration.
1. FastAPI: Schema-Driven OOP
FastAPI uses OOP strictly for data validation and structure, while using procedural functions for business logic.
Pydantic Models: FastAPI relies on pydantic classes to define the shape of your data. You write classes, but you do not fill them with complex logic or state-changing methods. They exist purely as type-checked data structures. [1, 2, 3] 
Functional Route Handlers: Your actual API endpoints are written as standard Python functions (def or async def), not methods inside a class.
Dependency Injection: FastAPI uses a functional dependency system (Depends()), which contrasts with the class-heavy dependency injection found in frameworks like NestJS or Spring Boot. [4] 
2. LangChain: Component-Driven OOP
LangChain is the most traditional OOP framework in this specific stack. It relies heavily on inheritance and the Factory Pattern. [5] 
Base Class Inheritance: When building custom tools, LLM wrappers, or memory systems, you explicitly inherit from LangChain base classes (e.g., inheriting from BaseTool or BaseChatModel). [6, 7, 8, 9] 
Runnable Interface: LangChain's Expression Language (LCEL) wraps everything into objects that implement a uniform interface (invoke, stream, batch). While it feels functional because you chain them with pipe | operators, it is fundamentally powered by underlying OOP abstractions. [10, 11, 12] 
3. LangGraph: State-Driven Functional/Graph Paradigm
LangGraph represents a major shift away from traditional OOP, leaning heavily toward Functional Programming and State Machines. [13, 14, 15] 
Centralized State: Instead of individual objects holding their own private data, LangGraph forces you to define a single, centralized State dict or Pydantic model.
Nodes as Pure Functions: The "Nodes" in your graph are typically just Python functions. They take the current state as an input, perform an action, and return an update to the state. They do not manipulate internal object variables.
Class Use Cases: You only use classes here to define the graph structure itself (using the StateGraph builder object) or to compile the graph into a runnable application. [16, 17, 18, 19, 20] 
Paradigm Summary Table
