Here are both comparisons to show you exactly how these software architecture paradigms work in production environments.
Part 1: The Shopping Cart (React Functional vs. Backend OOP)
This comparison highlights why the frontend loves immutability and pure functions, while the backend loves encapsulated state and business logic instances.
The React Functional Approach (Immutability & Hooks)
In React, state must never be modified directly. Instead, you create a brand-new copy of the state every time something changes to trigger a re-render.
import React, { useState } from 'react';
// Pure helper function - side-effect free, easy to test
const calculateTotal = (items) => 
  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);
export function ShoppingCartComponent() {
  // State is handled by a hook, not a class instance
  const [cartItems, setCartItems] = useState([]);
  const addItem = (newItem) => {
    setCartItems((prevItems) => {
      const existing = prevItems.find(item => item.id === newItem.id);
      if (existing) {
        // Enforce immutability: map creates a completely new array
        return prevItems.map(item =>
          item.id === newItem.id ? { ...item, quantity: item.quantity + 1 } : item
        );
      }
      return [...prevItems, { ...newItem, quantity: 1 }];
    });
  };
  return (
    <div>
      <h3>Total: ${calculateTotal(cartItems)}</h3>
      <button onClick={() => addItem({ id: 'p1', name: 'Shoes', price: 100 })}>
        Add Shoes
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
The Backend OOP Approach (Encapsulation & Domain Models)
On a Node.js or Python backend, your database record maps directly to a stateful object instance. The object protects its own data and exposes methods to alter that data.
// Classic Domain-Driven Design (DDD) Class
class CartItem {
  constructor(
    public readonly id: string,
    public readonly name: string,
    public readonly price: number,
    public quantity: number
  ) {}
}
export class ShoppingCart {
  // Encapsulation: The array is private. External code cannot modify it directly.
  private items: CartItem[] = [];
  public addItem(id: string, name: string, price: number): void {
    const existing = this.items.find(item => item.id === id);
    if (existing) {
      existing.quantity += 1; // Direct mutation is normal and expected here
    } else {
      this.items.push(new CartItem(id, name, price, 1));
    }
  }
  // Business logic lives inside the object that owns the data
  public getTotalPrice(): number {
    return this.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);
  }
  public getItems(): CartItem[] {
    return [...this.items]; // Return a copy to protect internal state encapsulation
  }
}
Part 2: FastAPI + LangGraph Integration Script
This minimal script demonstrates the modern Python ecosystem stack. It uses Pydantic/FastAPI classes for structural configuration and LangGraph functions for state transitions.
from fastapi import FastAPI
from pydantic import BaseModel
from typing import Annotated
from typing_extensions import TypedDict
from langgraph.graph import StateGraph, START, END
app = FastAPI()
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. DATA SCHEMA (Schema-Driven OOP via Pydantic)
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# We use classes to define data types and valid structures, not logic.
class AgentRequest(BaseModel):
    user_query: str
class AgentResponse(BaseModel):
    final_output: str
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. LANGGRAPH WORKFLOW (State-Driven Functional Paradigm)
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# The state is a plain data structure that moves through functions.
class GraphState(TypedDict):
    input_query: str
    agent_thought: str
    response: str
# Node 1: A pure function that processes state and returns an update
def analyzer_node(state: GraphState):
    query = state["input_query"].lower()
    return {"agent_thought": f"User is asking about: {query}"}
# Node 2: Another pure function processing the updated state
def responder_node(state: GraphState):
    thought = state["agent_thought"]
    return {"response": f"Processed successfully! [Reasoning: {thought}]"}
# Structural OOP: Using a class builder to compile the functional graph
workflow = StateGraph(GraphState)
workflow.add_node("analyzer", analyzer_node)
workflow.add_node("responder", responder_node)
workflow.add_edge(START, "analyzer")
workflow.add_edge("analyzer", "responder")
workflow.add_edge("responder", END)
compiled_graph = workflow.compile()
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. FASTAPI ROUTE HANDLER (Functional Route API)
# -------------------------------------------------------------
@app.post("/chat", response_model=AgentResponse)
async def handle_chat_request(payload: AgentRequest):
    # Prepare the initial state dict
    initial_state = {"input_query": payload.user_query}
    # Run the state graph synchronously or asynchronously
    result = compiled_graph.invoke(initial_state)
    # Return the targeted data from the final state back to the user
    return AgentResponse(final_output=result["response"])
Why this architecture works:
FastAPI handles the network boundaries using Pydantic classes to guarantee incoming and outgoing payloads are structured flawlessly.
LangGraph orchestrates execution data flow through pure functions (analyzer_node, responder_node), making agentic pipelines predictable, testable, and completely decoupled from complex base class overhead.
