Absolutely. The easiest way to understand these hooks is to first understand what problem each one solves.
1. The five hooks in simple language
2. useState — remember changing information
You already have this one.
const [price, setPrice] = useState(100);
Think:
"React, remember price for me."
When you do:
setPrice(200);
React remembers 200 and re-renders the component.
Use it when:
The value:
needs to be remembered, and
changing it should update the UI.
Examples:
price
loading
chatInput
chatMessages
isMenuOpen
currentPage
selectedUser
3. useEffect — do something after rendering
Think:
"After React renders, I want you to perform this action."
Example:
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, []);
The component renders → useEffect runs → data is fetched.
It's commonly used for things outside normal React rendering, such as:
API requests
event listeners
timers
subscriptions
interacting with browser APIs
For example:
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener("scroll", handleScroll);
  return () => {
    window.removeEventListener("scroll", handleScroll);
  };
}, []);
The cleanup function prevents the event listener from being left behind.
4. useMemo — remember a calculation
Suppose you have 1 million transactions.
const kpis = useMemo(() => {
  return calculateStats(transactions);
}, [transactions]);
Think:
"I've already calculated this. Don't calculate it again unless transactions changes."
Without useMemo:
Component renders
      ↓
calculateStats()
      ↓
Component renders again
      ↓
calculateStats() AGAIN
With useMemo:
Component renders
      ↓
calculateStats()
      ↓
Remember result
Component renders again
      ↓
Did transactions change?
      ↓
NO → use previous result
So:
useMemo remembers a VALUE/RESULT.
5. useRef — remember something without causing a render
const countRef = useRef(0);
You can change it:
countRef.current = 10;
But React doesn't re-render just because .current changed.
Think:
"React, keep this in a box for me, but don't update the screen whenever I change what's inside."
This is useful for:
Tracking values
const mouseX = useRef(0);
mouseX.current = newMousePosition;
Accessing DOM elements
const inputRef = useRef();
<input ref={inputRef} />
React Three Fiber
You can keep a reference to a Three.js object:
const meshRef = useRef();
and manipulate it directly.
So:
useRef remembers a VALUE/OBJECT without causing a re-render.
6. useCallback — remember a function
This is the one that is often confusing.
Suppose:
const handleClick = () => {
  console.log("Clicked");
};
Every time your component renders, JavaScript creates a new function.
Conceptually:
Render 1 → handleClick = Function A
Render 2 → handleClick = Function B
Render 3 → handleClick = Function C
Even though the function does exactly the same thing.
useCallback can remember the function:
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  console.log("Clicked");
}, []);
Now React can reuse the same function between renders unless its dependencies change.
Think:
"useCallback remembers a FUNCTION.
7. Why would you care about remembering a function?
It becomes particularly useful when passing functions to child components.
For example:
function Parent() {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("Clicked");
  }, []);
  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;
}
If Child is optimized with React.memo, keeping the same function reference can help prevent unnecessary child renders.
So useCallback is mainly a performance optimization, not something you need for every function.
8. useMemo vs useCallback
This is probably the most important comparison.
useMemo
Remembers a result/value:
const total = useMemo(() => {
  return price * quantity;
}, [price, quantity]);
Think:
"Remember the answer."
useCallback
Remembers a function:
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  console.log("Clicked");
}, []);
Think:
"Remember the function itself."
A useful way to remember:
useMemo     → remembers RESULT
useCallback → remembers FUNCTION
In fact, conceptually:
useCallback(fn, dependencies)
is similar to:
useMemo(() => fn, dependencies)
9. Now: What is a dependency array?
This is extremely important because you'll see it in several hooks.
A dependency array looks like this:
[price, quantity]
It tells React:
"These are the values that this code depends on. If one of them changes, do the relevant thing again."
For example:
useEffect(() => {
  console.log(price);
}, [price]);
The dependency is:
[price]
Meaning:
First render
     ↓
Run effect
price changes
     ↓
Run effect again
price doesn't change
     ↓
Don't run effect because of price
10. Three important dependency-array patterns
Pattern 1 — Empty array []
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, []);
This means:
"Run this effect when the component first mounts, and don't re-run it because of changing dependencies."
For example:
Component appears
       ↓
useEffect runs
       ↓
fetchData()
Component re-renders
       ↓
useEffect doesn't run again
Pattern 2 — Dependencies [value]
useEffect(() => {
  fetchUser(userId);
}, [userId]);
This means:
"Run this effect when userId changes."
For example:
userId = 10
    ↓
fetchUser(10)
userId = 20
    ↓
fetchUser(20)
Pattern 3 — No dependency array
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Hello");
});
This means the effect runs after every render.
Render → effect
Render → effect
Render → effect
Render → effect
This can be undesirable if the effect is expensive or causes another update.
11. Which hooks use dependency arrays?
The main ones you're learning are:
useEffect
useEffect(() => {
  fetchData();
}, [userId]);
Dependencies tell it when the effect should run again.
useMemo
const total = useMemo(() => {
  return calculateTotal(data);
}, [data]);
Dependencies tell it when the calculation should be done again.
useCallback
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(userId);
}, [userId]);
Dependencies tell it when the function needs to be recreated.
useState doesn't use a dependency array
const [price, setPrice] = useState(100);
It has an initial value, not a dependency array.
useRef doesn't use a dependency array
const inputRef = useRef(null);
It simply gives you a persistent reference.
12. A practical example using all five
Imagine your dashboard has transactions and a chat box.
function Dashboard() {
  const [transactions, setTransactions] = useState([]);
  const [chatInput, setChatInput] = useState("");
  const inputRef = useRef(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchTransactions().then(setTransactions);
  }, []);
  const kpis = useMemo(() => {
    return calculateKPIs(transactions);
  }, [transactions]);
  const sendMessage = useCallback(() => {
    sendToRAG(chatInput);
  }, [chatInput]);
  return (...);
}
Now look at what each one is doing:
useState
const [transactions, setTransactions] = useState([]);
Stores information.
useEffect
useEffect(() => {
  fetchTransactions();
}, []);
Performs an action after rendering.
useMemo
const kpis = useMemo(() => {
  return calculateKPIs(transactions);
}, [transactions]);
Remembers an expensive calculation.
useRef
const inputRef = useRef(null);
Keeps a reference/value without causing re-renders.
useCallback
const sendMessage = useCallback(() => {
  sendToRAG(chatInput);
}, [chatInput]);
Remembers a function.
13. The big picture
You can remember the five like this:
                    React Hooks
                         │
       ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
       │                 │                 │
    STORE              DO                 OPTIMIZE
       │                 │                 │
   useState          useEffect       ┌─────┴─────┐
       │                              │           │
   "Remember         "Do this       useMemo   useCallback
    this value"      after render"     │           │
                                    Remember     Remember
                                    result       function
                     useRef
                       │
              "Remember this thing
               without re-rendering"
The simplest possible definitions
useState → 🧠 Remember a value and update the UI when it changes.
useEffect → ⚡ Do an action after rendering/when dependencies change.
useMemo → 🧮 Remember an expensive calculation's result.
useRef → 📦 Remember a value/object without causing a re-render.
useCallback → 🧷 Remember a function so its reference doesn't unnecessarily change.
And the dependency array:
[thing1, thing2]
basically means:
"These are the things this code depends on. Pay attention to changes in them."
That's the core mental model you need before going deeper into React hooks.
